mu & Kashmir 154 502 83 261 1000 \nJharkhand 178 361 205 255 1000 \nKarnataka 211 328 264 196 1000 \nKerala 96 622 141 141 1000 \nMadhya Pradesh 125 491 230 152 1000 \nMaharashtra 145 581 128 147 1000 \nManipur 543 233 33 192 1000 \nMeghalaya 252 455 132 161 1000 \nMizoram 348 520 72 59 1000 \nNagaland 223 643 19 115 1000 \nOdisha 143 289 389 179 1000 \nPunjab 370 630 0 0 1000 \nRajasthan 141 466 214 179 1000 \nSikkim 394 357 0 249 1000 \nTamil Nadu 171 432 384 13 1000 \nTripura 122 397 9 472 1000 \nUttarakhand 74 288 0 638 1000 \nUttar Pradesh 220 627 69 84 1000 \nWest Bengal 100 518 179 203 1000 \nA & N Islands 0 825 0 175 1000 \nChandigarh 829 171 0 0 1000 \nDadra & Nagar \nHaveli \n243 663 94 0 1000 \nDaman & Diu 0 533 467 0 1000 \nLakshadweep 234 564 89 113 1000 \nPuducherry 0 1000 0 0 1000 \nAll India 195 465 180 160 1000 \nSource: NSS Report No.563: Employment and unemployment situation among social groups in India,  2011-12 \n\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_110": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.110 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.4.2016 \n \nFemale Literacy Rate \n \n110. SHRI ABHISHEK SINGH:  \nSHRI C.R. CHAUDHARY: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the steps taken by the Government to increase female literacy rate in the country including \nfunds allocated/utilized for the purpose during the last three years, State/UT -wise and year -\nwise;  \n(b) the details of achievements of the various Government sc hemes meant for girl child, like \nBeti Bachao Beti Padhao, PRAGATI etc. during the last three years, State/UT and year -wise; \nand  \n(c) the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to spread awareness and sensitize  \npeople regarding female education specially in rural areas in the country? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) In order to improve the female literacy rate in the country , “Saakshar Bharat”, a Centrally \nSponsored Scheme is being implemented in rural  areas of 410 districts  in 26 States and one \nUT that had adult female literacy rate of 50 per cent and below as per Census 2001, including \nleft wing extremism affected districts , irrespective of their literacy rates , with special focus \non women and other disadvantaged groups. In addition, a Centrally Sponsored Scheme Sarva \nShiksha Abhiyan (SSA) is also being implemented in conjunction with the Right of Children \nto Free and Compulsory Education Act, 2009 for universalisation of elementary education for \nall children in the 6-14 age group. A statement indicating State/UT -wise grants approved and \ncentral share released under Saakshar Bharat and SSA during the last three years are at \nAnnexure-I and Annexure-II respectively. \n(b) & (c) The Government is implementing Beti Bachao Beti Padhao (BBBP) programme  to \naddress the issue of decline in Child Sex Ratio (CSR) through a national campaign and \nfocussed multi sectoral action in 100 selected districts low in CSR, covering all States and \n\nUTs. Considering the criticality of the issue  and the success of the scheme, BBBP scheme is \nexpanded to additional 61 districts in 11 States/UTs. An exhaustive media campaign has been \nlaunched including Radio and TV campaign, Cinema Halls, BBBP messages through LPG \nBills, community engagement through Song & Drama Division and online digital media.   \n Under Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) several provisions viz. \nconstruction of separate toilets for girls, construction of residential quarters for teachers, \ncurriculum reforms, conducting self defence  training for the girls, special programme for \nempowerment of girls, stipend for girls with disabilities, vocationalis ation of secondary \neducation, girls hostel, etc. have been made for improving girls education and effec tively \naddressing female literacy rate. \nUnder the Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) several interventions viz. opening of schools \nin the neighbourhood to make access easier for girls, appointment of additional teachers \nincluding women teachers, separate toilets for girls, teachers’ sensitization programme to \npromote girls participation, gender sensitive teaching learning materials including textbooks  \nhave been introduced. In addition, Kasturba Gandhi Balika  Vidyalayas have been opened in \nEducationally Backward Blocks where the female rural literacy is below the national average \nto provide for residential upper primary schools for girls. \nUnder the Swachh Vidyalaya Initiative, the Government achieved the ambitious target \nof building 4,17,796 toilets in 2,61,400 government schools in one year alone by 15\nth August, \n2015. With this, about 13.58 crore children in 11.08 lakh government schools have access to \ngender segregated toilets. This has a positive impact on retention of girls in schools, which \nhas been recognized at the time of the Prime Minister’s Award of Excellence, 2016. \n*** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 110 FOR 25.4.2016 ASKED BY SHRI ABHISHEK \nSINGH AND SHRI C.R. CHAUDHARY REGARDING “FEMALE LITERACY \nRATE” \n \nState/UT-wise total grants (centre+state) approved and central share released under \nSaakshar Bharat programme  \n(Rs. in lakh) \nSl. \nNo.\n  \nState / UT \nTotal Grants \n(centre+state) \nApproved for \nproject period \nupto XII Plan  \nCentral share released \n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 46316.11 6921.76 3432 4456.45 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 7041.20 1417.84 1224.73 383.4 \n3 Assam 11151.36 1620 913.5 1319.76 \n4 Bihar 52882.42 6226.09 4788 3900 \n5 Chhasttisgarh 30804.96 1875 2250 1500 \n6 D&N Haveli 79.82 0 0 0 \n7 Gujarat 22149.83 0 0 1560 \n8 Haryana 9834.93 0 2546.35 0 \n9 Himachal Pradesh  1112.32 0 156 114.26 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 5501.31 0 0 725.4 \n11 Jharkhand 22518.42 0 0 1837.5 \n12 Karnataka 24848.05 1575.19 750 780 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 42275.03 0 2496 2620.8 \n14 Maharashtra 22744.09 0 3375 0 \n15 Manipur 1466.13 274.48 122.76 135 \n16 Meghalaya 1340.83 0 0 226.8 \n17 Nagaland 1455.63 198 256.47 151.93 \n18 Odisha 11998.80 228.16 0 624 \n19 Punjab 6939.26 0 0 0 \n20 Rajasthan 45173.10 4017 3215.54 0 \n21 Sikkim 239.54 66.8 0 74.88 \n22 Tamil Nadu 14190.90 2597.4 0 1209 \n23 Telangana 41406.95 Not in \nexistence 3432 1725 \n24 Tripura 355.42 0 0 72.54 \n25 Uttar Pradesh 145530.65 0 3978 7800 \n26 Uttarakhand 10707.97 1563.12 781.88 1216.8 \n27 West Bengal 16664.33 72.13 916.5 780 \n   Total 596729.42 28652.97 34634.73 33213.52 \n \n*** \n  \n\nAnnexure-II \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( a) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 110 FOR 25.4.2016 ASKED BY SHRI ABHISHEK \nSINGH AND SHRI C.R. CHAUDHARY REGARDING “FEMALE LITERACY \nRATE” \nState-wise Central Releases under Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (S