    "ls|LS16_S8_U_210": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n (MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*210 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.05.2016 \n \nTRIBAL CARNIVAL \n*210. SHRI MANOJ TIWARI:  \n Will the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)   whetheradequate steps have been taken to preserve, protect and promote ethnic culture of \nthe tribals, if so, the details, thereof; \n(b)  the details of various Centrally Sponsored schemes under implementation for the purpose;  \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to organize National Tribal Carnival in the near future; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof along with the aims and objectives of organizing such carnivals; and \n(e) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government for showcasing and promotion of \nvarious facets of tribal culture on a large scale in the country? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI JUAL ORAM) \n \n(a) to (e) A Statementis laid on the Table of the House \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (e) of the LokSabha  Starred Question No. \n*210 asked by SHRI MANOJ TIWARI to be answered on 09.05.2016  \n(a) &  (b) :Under Central Sector  scheme of “ Grant-in Aid to Tribal  Research Institutes \n(TRIs)”, Ministry of Tribal Affairs  extends financial assistance to TRIs for protection and \npromotion of traditional culture and languages of Scheduled Tribes . Functions of  TRIs, inter -\nalia, include organizing tribal festivals  for promotion of tribal culture. Ministry has also been \norganizing tribal festivals viz. ‘Prakriti’ and ‘Vanaj’ in past years, to showcase the tribal culture. \nTribal guests also take partin Republic Day celebrations so as topromote tribal culture and spread \nawareness about the same. \n“Development of Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups ” (PVTGs) scheme aims  at \nplanning socio-economic development in a comprehensive manner while retaining culture and \nheritage of PVTGs.Further, Tribal Cooperative Marketing Development Federation of India Ltd. \n(TRIFED) organizes exhibitions like ‘Aadichitra’ and ‘Aadishilp’where tribals showcase their \nrich tribal heritage of art and crafts . TRIFED also participates in  OCTAVE – a dance festival of \nNorth Eastern Region.  \n \n(c) & (d):Yes, Madam. Tribal Carnival aims to show-case tribal ways of life including their \nculture, tradition and customs. U nderlying idea is to preserve and promote traditional culture \nthrough display of various facets of tribal life relating to culture, tradition, customs and skills. \nThis also aims to instill a sense of pride amongst tribals for their rich cultur e heritage.  Tribal \nartists and artisans get national level stage for exhibiting their talent. Thisalso facilitatesvisitorsto \nmake themselves aware about unique and diverse traditional culture of  tribal population in real \nperspective.  \n \n(e):Besides, Ministry of Culture has  also been releasing funds under Tribal Sub -Plan to various \ninstitutions of Central Government for protection and promotion of traditional culture and \nlanguages of Scheduled Tribes. The intervention includes activities of Zonal Cultu re Centres, \nAnthropological Survey of India (Kolkata), Indi ra Gandhi RashtriyaManavSangrahalaya \n(Bhopal), Indira Gandhi National Centre for the Arts, Central Institute of Buddhist Studies (Leh), \nLalit Kala Akademi etc. The State level Tribal Festival s are also specifically supported  for \nshowcasing and promotion of various facets of tribal culture. \n**** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_211": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.211\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.05.2016\n \n \n \nBRINGING BACK ANTIQUE ITEMS FROM ABROAD\n \n \n*211.   SHRI MULLAPPALLY RAMACHANDRAN:\n \n \nSHRI RAKESH SINGH:\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n       \n \n \n       \n \nWill the \nMinister of CULTURE\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \nwhether the Government has made any list of antique items of national \nimportance including ‘Kohinoor’  diamond which are kept abroad in \nmuseums of different countries;\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government \nhas made any efforts to bring back those items \nincluding  the ‘Kohinoor’;\n \n(d)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government in this \nregard; and \n \n(e)\n \nthe details of legal and technical hurdles in bringing back these items?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMIN\nISTER OF STATE, CULTURE AND TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) AND \nCIVIL AVIATION\n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n(a) to (e)   A statement is laid on the table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.211 F\nOR 09.05.2016 REGARDING \nBRINGING BACK \nANTIQUE ITEMS FROM ABROAD\n \n \n \n \n(a) to\n \n(b)\n \nNo, Madam.\n \n(c) to (d)\n \nThe Ministry of External Affairs is exploring ways and means for \nobtaining a satisfactory resolution to this issue with the UK \nGovernment. \n \n(e)\n \nThe \nobjects taken away from the country prior to independence \nincluding Kohinoor diamond do not fall under the purview of \nAntiquities and Art Treasures Act, 1972.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_212": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.212 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 09.05.2016 \n \n \nLiteracy \n \n \n*212. SHRI RAVNEET SINGH:  \n SHRI JITENDRA CHAUDHURY: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the UNESCO Report 2015 reveals that a considerable Indian population is \nilliterate and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the Government has conducted any survey to assess literate population across the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof during the last three years, State/UT and year-wise;  \n(c) the ratio between literate and illiterate people in the country, State/UT-wise;  \n(d) whether the Government has signed any agreement with any Non-Governmental \nOrganisations or foreign countries for promoting literacy in the country and if so, the details \nthereof; and  \n(e) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government to improve literacy rate in the \ncountry? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n \n \n  \n\n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 212 FO R 9.5.2016 ASKED BY SHRI RAVNEET \nSINGH AND SHRI JITENDRA CHAUDHURY REGARDING “LITERACY”. \n \n(a) As per the “Education for All: Global Monitoring Report 2015” published by UNESCO, \naround 28.55 crore adult illiterates are reported in India.  They constitute 36.5 per cent of \nworld illiterates. \n(b) & (c) The literacy data in the country is collected through decennial census operation \nconducted by Registrar General of India. Th e last census was held in 2011. A statement \nindicating State/UT-wise number of  literates, literacy and illiteracy rates, in 7 and above age \ngroup, as reported by Census 2011 is annexed. \n(d) No Madam. \n(e) In order to improve literacy rate, Saakshar Bharat, a Centrally Sponsored Scheme for \nAdult Education and Skill Development is being implemented in rural areas of 410 districts \nin 26 States and one UT that had adult female literacy rate of 50 per cent and below as per \nCensus 2001, and including left wing extremism affected districts, irrespective of their \nliteracy rates, with special focus on women and other disadvantaged groups. In addition, \nSarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA), a Centrally Sponsored Scheme is also being implemented in \nconjunction with the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act, 2009 for \nuniversalisation of elementary education for all children in the 6-14 age group.  \n*** \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) & (c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 212 FOR 9.5.2016 ASKED BY SHRI RAVNEET SINGH \nAND SHRI JITENDRA CHAUDHURY REGARDING “LITERACY”. \n \nState/UT-wise literates, literacy and illiteracy rates, in the age group of 7 and above \n(As per Census 2011) \nSl. No India/States/UTs No. of  \nLiterates Literacy rate Illiteracy \nrate \n  INDIA        76,36,38,812 72.98 27.02 \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH          5,05,56,760 67.02 32.98 \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH              7,66,005 65.38 34.62 \n3 ASSAM          1,91,77,977 72.19 27.81 \n4 BIHAR          5,25,04,553 61.80 38.2 \n5 CHHATTISGARH          1,53,79,922 70.28 29.72 \n6 GOA            11,65,487 88.70 11.3 \n7 GUJARAT          4,10,93,358 78.03 21.97 \n8 HARYANA          1,65,98,988 75.55 24.45 \n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH            50,39,736 82.80 17.2 \n10 JAMMU & KASHMIR            70,67,233 67.16 32.84 \n11 JHARKHAND          1,83,28,069 66.41 33.59 \n12 KARNATAKA          4,06,47,322 75.36 24.64 \n13 KERALA          2,81,35,824 94.00 6 \n14 MADHYA PRADESH          4,28,51,169 69.32 30.68 \n15 MAHARASHTRA          8,15,54,290 82.34 17.66 \n16 MANIPUR            19,08,476 76.94 23.06 \n17 MEGHALAYA            17,85,005 74.43 25.57 \n18 MIZORAM              8,48,175 91.33 8.67 \n19 NAGALAND            13,42,434 79.55 20.45 \n20 ODISHA          2,67,42,595 72.87 27.13 \n21 PUNJAB          1,87,07,137 75.84 24.16 \n22 RAJASTHAN          3,82,75,282 66.11 33.89 \n23 SIKKIM              4,44,952 81.42 18.58 \n24 TAMIL NADU          5,18,37,507 80.09 19.91 \n25 TRIPURA            28,04,783 87.22 12.78 \n26 UTTAR PRADESH        11,43,97,555 67.68 32.32 \n27 UTTARAKHAND            68,80,953 78.82 21.18 \n28 WEST BENGAL          6,15,38,281 76.26 23.74 \n29 ANDAMAN & NICOBAR ISLANDS              2,94,281 86.63 13.37 \n30 CHANDIGARH              8,05,438 86.05 13.95 \n31 DADRA & NAGAR HAVELI              2,23,230 76.24 23.76 \n32 DAMAN & DIU              1,88,406 87.10 12.9 \n33 LAKSHADWEEP                 52,553 91.85 8.15 \n34 NCT OF DELHI          1,27,37,767 86.21 13.79 \n35 PUDUCHERRY              9,57,309 85.85 14.15 \n \n*** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_213": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.213 \nANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nHIMALAYAN TOURIST CIRCUIT \n \n213. SHRI AJAY TAMTA:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to form a Himal ayan Tourist \ncircuit for development of tourism in Himalayan States in the \ncountry;  \n(b)  if so, the locations proposed to be connected under this circuit, \nState-wise;  \n(c)  whether the Jageshwar Dham situated at Almora and the Mahakali \ntemple situated at Gangolihat in Pithoragarh in Uttarakhand are also \nproposed to be developed as world class tourist destinations;  \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to give priority to connect the \nsaid religious places with the tourist circuits in India to bring them at \nworld-level; and  \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n(a) to (e): The Ministry of Tourism has launched the Swadesh Darshan \nScheme in 2014-15 with a vision to develop theme based t ourist circuits in \nthe country on the principles of high tourist value, competitiveness and \nsustainability in an integrated manner by synergizing efforts to focus on \nneeds and concerns of all stakeholders to enrich tourist experience and \nenhance employment opportunities.  \n \nHimalayan Circuit is one of the circuits identified for development \nunder this scheme.  Himalayan Circuit includes the States of Jammu and \nKashmir, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand and North Eastern Regions.  \n \nMinistry of Tourism provides Ce ntral Financial Assistance for \nprojects identified in consultation with the State Governments/UTs for \ndevelopment of tourist infrastructure under this scheme. The projects are \nsanctioned subject to availability of funds, liquidation of pending \nutilization certificates, submission of suitable Detailed Project Report \n(DPR) and adherence to the scheme guidelines. \n********* ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_214": " GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 214 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nWomen in Higher Education \n214. SHRIMATI VANAROJA R.: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has any  proposal to give additional time to women and \ndifferently abled persons to complete their M.Phil and Ph.D programme; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether it is true that many women are getting enrolled in higher education but  not \nmany are seen in faculty of colleges and universities; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the remedial measures being adopted by the \nGovernment in this regard; and \n(e) whether the female students would be given maternity leave of 240 days which would \nbe excluded from their total time of  studies and if so, the details thereof and the action \ntaken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a): Yes, Madam.  \n(b):  The University Grants Commission (UGC) has approved that women candidates  \nand persons with disability (more than 40% disability) may be allowed a relaxation of one \nyear for M.Phil. and two years for Ph.D in the maximum duration of these courses.  \n(c) to (e): The All India Survey on Higher Education (AISHE) provisional report for the \nyear 2014-15 has estimated t he total number of teachers to be 14,18,389 out of which \n39% are women.  This report has estimated that during 2014- 15 women students \nconstituted 39% of the total enrolment in Ph.D programme in higher education institutions \nin the country.  \nA Ph.D degree is one of the minimum qualifications for recruitment to the post of \nAssistant Professor in Universities and Colleges. The UGC has initiated several \nmeasures to promote enrolment of women in Ph .D courses . The measures include \namendments in the Ph.D Regulations by relaxing the maximum duration for completion \nof Ph .D or MPhil.  by women candidates for  a further two years  and one year, \nrespectively; allowing transfer of research data in case of relocation of an M.Phil/Ph.D \nwoman scholar due to marr iage or otherwise; and allowing women candidates maternity \n\nleave/child-care leave once in the entire duration of M.Phil./Ph.D for up to 240 days.  \nFurther, t he UGC is implementing p ost-doctoral fellowship for women c andidates (un-\nemployed); Swami Vivekananda single girl c hild scholarship for research in s ocial \nsciences  and PG Indira Gandhi Scholarship for single girl child for postgraduate courses \nto promote the enrolment of women at post- doctoral, doctoral and its feeder levels.  \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_215": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 215 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nRanking of Universities \n \n215. SHRI RAOSAHEB DANVE PATIL: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is true that Indian Universities/Colleges have been successful in \nfinding place in the top twenty list of leading small universities;  \n(b) if so,  the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is taking any steps to develop other small \nuniversities/colleges to make them competitive an international level; and \n(d) if so, the details and the outcome thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n(a) & (b):  The Times Higher Education rankings has brought out the World Small \nUniversity Rankings in 2016 naming therein Indian Institute of Technology, \nGuwahati (ranked 14) and Savitribai Phule Pune University (ranked 18) in the top \n20.  \n(c) & (d): With a view to improving the competitiveness of the higher educational \ninstitutions, the National Institutional Ranking Framework (NIRF)  has been \nlaunched on September 29, 2015. The NIRF rankings are based on clearly verifiable \nobjective criteria covering five broad para meters. The NIRF is expected to \nbenchmark good performing institutions and would help others to understand \ntheir areas of strength and weakness and to take corrective action as necessary. \nThe first India Rankings 2016 have been announced on 04.04.2016 and are \navailable at https://www.nirfindia.org/Home . \n \n********* \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_216": "1\n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.216\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 9.5.2016\n \n \nVAISAKHA 19, 1938 (SAKA)\n \n \n \nADARSH SMARAK YOJANA \n \n \n \n*216.\n \nSHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN: \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether t\nhe Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) proposes to implement a \nproject ‘Adarsh Smarak’; \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof including the number of monuments identified by the \nASI to implement the said project, State/UT\n-\nwise; \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government is \nplanning to upgrade all the monuments managed \nby  ASI to the standards of the Adarsh Smarak in a phased manner; and \n \n(d)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the time by which these monuments are likely to \nbe upgraded?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE, CULTURE AND\n \nTOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) AND \nMINISTER OF STATE, CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n \n(a)to(d) \n \nA statement is laid on the table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n2\n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERR\nED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d\n) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 216 FOR 9\n.5.2016\n \n \n \n(a)\n \n‘Adarsh Smarak’ scheme has already been implemented.\n \n \n(b)\n \nThe details of 25 centrally protected monuments identified and included in \nthe list of ‘Adarsh Smarak’ in the country (State/UT\n-\nwise) is at Annexure. \n \n \n(c)&(d)\n \nNo, Madam.  Besides, b\nasic tourist amenities, Adarsh Monuments are \nprovided with additional facilities like Wi\n-\nFi, cafeteria, interpretation centre, \nbrail signage, modern toilets etc. However, these identified facilities would be \nmonument specific on the basis of actual require\nment and feasibility on case \nto case basis. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n3\n \n \n \n \n \nANNEXURE\n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 216 FOR 9.5.2016\n \n \nDETAILS OF PROTECTED MONUMENTS IDENTIFIED AND INCLUDED IN THE LIST \nOF ADARSH SMARA\nK IN VARIOUS STATES OF THE COUNTRY \n \n \nSL.NO.\n \nNAME OF MONUMENT\n \n \nSTATE\n \n1\n \nRang Ghar, Sibsagar\n \nAssam\n \n2\n \nVaishali\n-\n \nKolhua\n \nBihar\n \n3\n \nHumayun’s Tomb\n \n \nDelhi\n \n4\n \nQutb Complex\n \n5\n \nRed Fort\n \n6\n \nRani\n-\nki\n-\nVav, Patan\n \nGujarat\n \n7\n \nRock\n-\ncut Temples, Masroor\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n8\n \nLeh Palace\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n9\n \nMartand Temple\n \n10\n \nHampi, Bellary\n \nKarnataka\n \n11\n \nPattadakal Group of Monuments \n \n12\n \nElephanta Caves, Mumbai \n \nMaharashtra\n \n13\n \nDaultabad Fort\n \n14\n \nMandu \n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n15\n \nKhajuraho\n \n16\n \nKonark Temple\n \nOdisha\n \n17\n \nKumbhalgarh F\nort, Kelwada\n \nRajasthan\n \n18\n \nShore Temple Mahabalipuram\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n19\n \nBrihadeshwar Temple, Thanjavur\n \n20\n \nTaj Mahal, Agra\n \n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n21\n \nGroup of Monuments at Fatehpur Sikri, Agra\n \n22\n \nSravasti\n \n23\n \nSarnath, Varanasi\n \n24\n \nJageshwar Group of Temples\n \nUtta\nrakhand\n \n25\n \nHazardwari Palace, Murshidabad\n \nWest Bengal\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_217": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�\n�������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�\n�������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������ �\n���������������������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n������������������������������������� �\n�\n����� ���� ��������� ��� ������� ���� ����������� ��� �������� ���\n������ �\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������� �\n����������� ���� ����������� ���� �������� ����� ��� ���������� ��� �������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������ �\n������� ������� ����������� ��� ���������� ���� ����������� ��� ���� �����\n������������������������� �\n������������������������ ���������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������ �\n�\n������� �\n������������������������������������������������ �\n����� �������������������� �\n�\n����� �������������� ��� ������ ������ ���� ������� ������� ��������� �����������\n������� ����� ��� ���������� ������� ��������� ��������� �������������\n��������� ������������ �������� ������ ������� ���� �������������\n��������� ��������� �������� ������ �������� �� �������� �������� �������\n�������������������������������� �\n��������� ��\n\n����������\n����� ������������� ����������� ��� ������ �������� ����� ������� ���������\n���������� ����� ���������� ��� �� ������������� ����� ������ ����� �����\n���������������� �������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ���� ��������� ��� ������� ��������������� �������� �������� ��������\n������������������������������������������� �\n��� ���������������������������������������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n�����������������������