    "ls|LS16_S8_U_80": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n(MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.80 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25.04.2016 \n \nBRANDING OF TRIBALS \n \n80. SHRIMATI RANJEET RANJAN:   \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the cumulative number of tribal children branded in various States of the country along \nwith the names of the States having the highest number of tribal population; \n(b) whether the Government has taken any measures to curb such incidents and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(c) the total number of Primary Health Centres (PHCs) in the tribal regions of such States \nincluding posts of doctors in PHCs filled during the last year, State/UT -wise along \nwith the total number of Anganwadi and ASHA workers deployed in the tribal \ndominated regions of the country, State/UT-wise; \n(d) whether the Ministry has a headcount of traditional healers in various States across the \ncounty and if so, the details thereof and if not, whether the Ministry has any plan to do \nso; and \n(e) whether the Government proposes to increase a wareness amongst tribal population \nabout the harmful effects of branding and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI JUAL ORAM) \n \n(a) & (b) The States of Madhya Pradesh, Maharas htra, Odisha, Rajasthan, Gujarat, \nJharkhand and Chhattisgarh have  major concentration  of tribal population in the \ncountry. The  information relating cumulative number of children , if any, branded \nin States,  is not centrally maintain ed by the Ministry of Tribal Affairs.  There are \nstatutory  Acts/guidelines of Government  of India  for protecting rights of children.  \n(c )  The details of total number of Primary Health Centres (PHCs) in the tribal areas \n(State- wise), doctors strength in these areas and number of Anganwadi and ASHA \nworkers deployed in the country including the tribal areas is at Annexure-I.  \n \n(d) The details of traditional healers in the country are not available centrally with the \nMinistry of Tribal Affairs. However, the Ministry is propagating  to the States/UTs the \ndocumentation of  tribal  medicine  and  medicinal plants  / practices  not only as very \nuseful medical practices but also for the sak e of bio-diversity and also  for preservation   \nof  India’s   rich heritage.   \n(e) Government of India  from time to time through print, electronic and social media  \nprovides awareness  for various programmatic /schematic  interventions  on pan India \nbasis  including for the tribal population. This includes awareness /advisories on health \nissues and for utilizing the health infrastructure etc.   \n \n**** \n  \n\n \nStatement referred to in reply to part (c) of LSUSQ No. 80 for 25.4.2016 on “Branding of \nTribals”.                                                    \n     \nNUMBER OF  PHCs  IN TRIBAL AREAS  \nS.No. State/ UT \n(As on 31st March, 2015) \nTribal \nPopulation in \nRural Areas \nPHCs \n1 Andhra Pradesh 2293102 130 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh #  789846 117 \n3 Assam 3665405 283 \n4 Bihar+ 1270851 6 \n5 Chhattisgarh 7231082 411 \n6 Goa   87639 8 \n7 Gujarat 8021848 382 \n8 Haryana  * 0 0 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 374392 43 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 1406833 48 \n11 Jharkhand 7868150 160 \n12 Karnataka 3429791 64 \n13 Kerala 433092 137 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 14276874 332 \n15 Maharashtra 9006077 315 \n16 Manipur^ 791126 45 \n17 Meghalaya  # 2136891 110 \n18 Mizoram   # 507467 57 \n19 Nagaland  # 1306838 128 \n20 Odisha++ 8994967 426 \n21 Punjab  * 0 0 \n22 Rajasthan 8693123 210 \n23 Sikkim 167146 12 \n24 Tamil Nadu 660280 66 \n25 Telangana 2939027 93 \n26 Tripura 1117566 45 \n27 Uttarakhand 264819 10 \n28 Uttar Pradesh 1031076 NA \n29 West Bengal 4855115 304 \n30 A&N Island  26715 4 \n31 Chandigarh  * 0 0 \n32 D&N Haveli  # 150944 7 \n33 Daman & Diu 7617 0 \n34 Delhi  * 0 0 \n35 Lakshadweep#(2) 13463 4 \n36 Puducherry  * 0 0 \n  India 93819162 3957 \nNotes: The requirement 