    "ls|LS16_S8_U_81": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  81 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 25th APRIL, 2016 \n \nScope of Right to Education \n \n81. SHRI ANANDRAO ADSUL:  \n SHRI DHARMENDRA YADAV:  \n SHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO RAUT:  \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has set up a Group of Secretaries on Education and Health to \nexamine the scope of Right to Education Act in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof ;  \n(c) whether the said Committee has submitted its report and if so, the details of recommendations \nmade therein; and  \n(d) whether the Government has examined the recommendations of the said Committee and if so, \nthe details thereof ? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n \n(a): No, Madam.  \n \n(b) to (d): Do not arise.  \n \n* * * \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S8_U_82": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 82\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 29TH APRIL, 2016\nBAN OF FDC DRUGS\n*82. SHRI ASHOK SHANKARRAO CHAVAN:\nSHRIMATI KOTHAPALLI GEETHA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has tested more than 1200 Fixed Dose Combination (FDC)\ndrugs and prohibited the manufacture, sale and distribution of abou t 350 drugs, if so, the\ndetails of such drugs/ medicines and the reasons therefor;\n(b) whether the pharma companies have opposed the ban, if so, the reasons therefor and\nthe response of the Government thereto;\n(c) whether the Government has taken note of side effects of such banned FDC\ndrugs/medicines and complaints of smuggling of contraband drugs, if so, the details\nthereof; and\n(d) the corrective action taken by the Government to ensure safety of consumers and allow\nmanufacture, sale and distribution of safe medicines having therapeutic justification?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 82* FOR 29TH APRIL, 2016\n(a) The Government vide Gazette Notifications S.O. Nos. 705(E) to 1048(E) dated\n10.03.2016 prohibited the manufacture for sale, sale and distribution for human use 344\nFDCs with immediate effect in public interest as these FDCs were likely to involve risk to\nhuman beings and saf er alternatives were available. The list of FDCs prohibited is\nAnnexed.\n(b) The Pharma companies have opposed prohibition inter alia on the following\nprocedural grounds :\n(i). Government did not consult Drugs Technical Advisory Board (DTAB)/\nDrugs Consultative Committee (DCC).\n(ii). Opportunity was not given to the manufacturers.\n(iii). The manufacturers were not heard after they replied to show cause\nnotices.\n(iv). The notification is vague and merely says there is no therapeutic\njustification and other safer alternatives are available.  It does not specify\nwhich are those alternatives.\n(v). The products are in the market for a long time and there are no adverse\nreaction, etc.\n(vi). The manufacturers are having some kind of licence from State Licensing\nAuthority before September,  1988 o r are having some kind of\nNOC/approval from the Drugs Controller General (India) [DCG(I)].\n(vii). Similar products are approved by DCG(I).\n(viii). Government ban will make the stockists/ retailers, etc. liable for\nprosecution under section 18.\n(ix). The petitioners are having valid licenses from State Licensing Authorities.\nIn the context of the foregoing, it needs to be noted that Fixed Dose Combinations\n(FDCs) containing drugs combined together for the first time are treated as ‘New Drugs’.\n\nThese, therefore, require permission from the DCG(I) before these could be licensed by the\nState Licensing Authorities (SLAs) for manufacture for sale in the country.  Many SLAs\nhad, despite not having the authority to grant licences for new FDCs, continue d to grant\nlicences without approval of the DCG(I).  In order to address this issue, the Department of\nHealth and Family Welfare also issued statutory directions to the State/Union Territory\nGovernments to instruct their respective drugs licensing authorities to refrain from granting\nsuch licenses. However, the practice was still not discontinued by some SLAs.  The matter\nhad also been considered by the Standing Committee of Parliament which had observed\nthat Section 26A of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 is adequate to deal with the\nproblem of irrational and/or FDCs not cleared by CDSCO. Though, the licenses\nissued/obtained were without jurisdiction and therefore not legal, keeping in view the\npublic health and safety concerns, on the direction of the Gov ernment, CDSCO on\n15.01.2013, requested the State Regulators to ask the concerned manufacturers in their\nStates to prove the safety and efficacy of such FDCs which had been licensed by SLAs\nprior to 01.10.2012 without approval of DCG(I) before the office of DCG(I) within a\nperiod of 18 months. It was informed that in the event of failure to prove safety and\nefficacy, such FDCs would be considered for being prohibited for manufacture and\nmarketing in the country. This notice was also uploaded on the officia l website of CDSCO\nas an information and for necessary action. Subsequent to it, CDSCO also issued a letter\ndated 05.07.2013 to the State Drug Controllers asking the manufacturers to file\napplications as per the procedure defined in the letter for proving the safety and efficacy of\nthese FDCs. In reply to above letter, CDSCO received approximately 6320 applications\nfrom the manufacturers till 14th July 2014. Keeping in view the fact that rationality of such\ndrugs had not been proved, in the larger public i nterest, and to examine these applications\nin a timely manner, the Ministry constituted a Committee under the Chairmanship of Prof.\nC. K. Kokate, Vice-Chancellor, KLE University, Belgaum, Karnataka on 16.09.2014.\nThe Chairman of the Committee along with the members presented the report before\nthe Ministry on 04.03.2015 wherein the Committee was requested to further examine and\nelaborate the scientific reasons for each FDC considered as Irrational by the Committee\nand submit the report accordingly. The Com mittee held a large number of FDCs to be\n\nrational and these were allowed to be manufactured/marketed. Further, meetings of the\nCommittee took place on 07.04.2015 and 08.04.2015 and Committee gave scientific\nreasons in respect of each FDC/s held to be irrat ional. Thereafter, the final report of the\nCommittee was submitted to the Ministry on 16.4.2015.\nAfter examination of the recommendations of the Committee in case of FDCs\ndeclared irrational, show cause notices were issued and opportunity was again affor ded to\nthe applicants to respond within 30 days. However, on the request of the manufacturers,\nfurther extension of 90 days was granted for submitting their replies. The replies received\nwere thereafter examined by the Expert Committee and further recommen dations were\ngiven on 10.02.2016 after thorough examination of all replies. The experts in relevant areas\nalso assisted the Committee in the evaluation process.\nThe  above findings of the Expert Committee were inter alia based on various\nparameters such as patient safety, drug toxicity/adverse effect, misuse of drug/prescription\nerror, abuse potential, pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic interaction/compatibility,\ndosage compatibilities of FDCs vis -à-vis that of single ingredients, issue of antimicrobial\ndrug resistance, latest standard treatment guidelines (STG), risk/benefit ratio, patient\ncompliance, international status, etc. The matter was, thereafter , further examined by the\nGovernment and the FDCs found to be irrational were prohibited. Thus, the Expe rt\nCommittee has carried out an elaborate exercise based on contemporary scientific\nknowledge before finalising its recommendations. Adequate opportunity has been\nprovided to the manufacturers to place their position before the Committee and the\nGovernment.\n(c) All facts related to these FDCs were duly considered by the Government before taking\na decision.\n(d) While, the FDCs have been prohibited, a large number of cases have been filed by the\nPharmaceutical Manufacturers in Delhi, Chennai, Himachal Pradesh and Karnatka.   Delhi\nand Karnatka High Courts have granted stay in cases filed in these courts and the matter is\ncurrently sub-judice.\n\nList of FDCs prohibited\nGSR\nNO.\nS.No. Name of FDC\n1 Aceclofenac + Paracetamol + Rabeprazole S.O.\n705\n(E)\n2 Nimesulide  + Diclofenac S.O.\n706\n(E)\n3 Nimesulide  + Cetirizine  +Caffeine S.O.\n707\n(E)\n4 Nimesulide +Tizanidine S.O.\n708\n(E)\n5 Paracetamol + cetrizine + caffeine S.O.\n709\n(E)\n6 Diclofenac   + Tramadol   + Chlorzoxazone S.O.\n710\n(E)\n7 Dicyclomine  +Paracetamol +Domperidone S.O.\n711\n(E)\n8 Nimesulide +Paracetamol  dispesible tablets S.O.\n712\n(E)\n9 Paracetamol + Phenylephrine  + Caffeine S.O.\n713\n(E)\n10 Diclofenac +Tramadol +Paracetamol S.O.\n714\n(E)\n11 Diclofenac + paracetamol + chlorzoxazone + famotidine S.O.\n715\n(E)\n12 Naproxen+Paracetamol S.O.\n716\n(E)\n13 Nimesulide + serratiopeptidase S.O.\n717\n(E)\n\n14 Paracetamol +Diclofenac  +Famotidine S.O.\n718\n(E)\n15 Nimesulide + Pitofenone + Fenpiverinium  + benzyl alcohol S.O.\n719\n(E)\n16 Omeprazole + Paracetamol + Diclofenac S.O.\n720\n(E)\n17 Nimesulide  + Paracetamol injection S.O.\n721\n(E)\n18 Tamsulosin  + diclofenac S.O.\n722\n(E)\n19 Paracetamol +Phenylephirine  +Chlorpheniramine  +Dextromethorphan  +Caffeine S.O.\n723\n(E)\n20 Diclofenac  +Zinc Carnosine S.O.\n724\n(E)\n21 Diclofenac  + paracetamol+ chlorpheniramine maleate + magnesium trisillicate S.O.\n725\n(E)\n22 Paracetamol + pseudoephdrine + cetrizine S.O.\n726\n(E)\n23 Phenylbutazone+Sodium Salicylate S.O.\n727\n(E)\n24 Lornoxicam+Paracetamol+Trypsin S.O.\n728\n(E)\n25 Paracetamol +Mefenamic Acid +Ranitidine +Dicyclomine S.O.\n729\n(E)\n26 Nimesulide  + Dicyclomine S.O.\n730\n(E)\n27 Heparin  +Diclofenac S.O.\n731\n(E)\n28 Glucosamine  +Methyl Sulfonyl Methane+Vitamin D3 + Manganese + Boron+\nCopper +Zinc S.O.\n732\n(E)\n\n29 Paracetamol +Tapentadol S.O.\n733\n(E)\n30 Tranexamic Acid  + Proanthocyanidin S.O.\n734\n(E)\n31 Benzoxonium Chloride+Lidocaine S.O.\n735\n(E)\n32 Lornoxicam +Paracetamol +Tramadol S.O.\n736\n(E)\n33 Lornoxicam +Paracetamol +Serratiopeptidase S.O.\n737\n(E)\n34 Diclofenac  +Paracetamol +Magnesium trisilicate S.O.\n738\n(E)\n35 Paracetamol +Domperidone +Caffeine S.O.\n739\n(E)\n36 Ammonium Chloride +Sodium Citrate +Chlorpheniramine Maleate +Menthol S.O.\n740\n(E)\n37 Paracetamol +Prochlorperazine Maleate S.O.\n741\n(E)\n38 Combikit of 3 tablets of Serratio